      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll read the original test and the CUT source to understand what needs to be improved.Now I understand the test. Let me write a more understandable version.\n\nThe test builds a 3-byte array with the `ACCESS_TIME_BIT` flag (value `2`) at index 2, then calls `parseFromLocalFileData` with `offset=2, length=2`. This causes the parser to read the flags byte (value `2`) at `data[2]`, but there is insufficient remaining data to read the access timestamp itself. The assertion verifies that `getFlags()` still returns `2` — the raw flags byte is preserved even when the corresponding timestamp data is absent.The improved test compiled successfully. The refactored version replaces magic bytes with the named constant `X5455_ExtendedTimestamp.ACCESS_TIME_BIT`, renames cryptic variables to `extendedTimestamp` and `localFileData`, and adds a Javadoc comment explaining the boundary condition being tested (flags byte preserved even when the corresponding timestamp data is too short to read)."
    }
